CAT = 'sgm9Service'; SEG = 'SRV'
Q = [
('A proposer is unsure whether a past illness matters to the insurer. Under the duty of good faith, the proposer should:',
 'Disclose it anyway', ['Leave it out', 'Mention it at claim time', 'Tell only the agent verbally'],
 'Life insurance relies on good faith. LIA advises disclosing a fact when unsure whether it is important, and putting it in the proposal form.'),
('Under the LIA Code, the insurer decides to accept, counter-offer or decline an application within:',
 '30 days of having all information', ['7 days of receiving the proposal form', '90 days of the proposal', '14 days of the medical exam'],
 "The LIA Code sets 30 days from receipt of all relevant information. Getting doctors' reports can make the whole process take longer."),
('An insurer offers cover with an exclusion instead of standard terms. Before the policy can be issued:',
 'The proposer must accept the terms', ['The policy starts at once', 'MAS must approve the exclusion', 'The agent accepts for the client'],
 "Modified terms are a counter-offer. The insurer needs the proposer's acceptance of those terms before it issues the policy."),
('Between handing in a signed proposal with the first premium and the policy starting, most insurers give interim cover against:',
 'Death by accident', ['Death from any cause', 'Any critical illness', 'Hospital stays'],
 'Most life policies give temporary cover for death caused directly and solely by an accident in this period, with the premium held as a conditional deposit.'),
('A proposer with a health condition is accepted on a higher-than-standard rating. This is usually done by:',
 'Adding an extra premium loading', ['Cutting the free-look period', 'Removing the grace period', 'Paying a lower bonus rate'],
 'Underwriters can accept higher risks with a premium loading, an exclusion or a lower sum assured, instead of declining outright.'),
('A policy is posted to the client. Under the LIA Code, the 14-day free-look period starts:',
 'Seven days after posting', ['On the date of posting', 'On the date of the proposal', 'When the first premium is paid'],
 'The free-look runs for at least 14 days from receipt of the policy, and a posted policy is treated as received seven days after posting.'),
('A whole life policy is cancelled within the free-look period. The client generally gets back:',
 'Premiums less medical costs', ['Premiums plus interest', 'The full sum assured', 'Nothing after the first day'],
 'The insurer refunds premiums paid without interest, less expenses it incurred such as the medical examination fee.'),
('Under the LIA Code, the insurer sends the new policy document within:',
 '30 days of issuing the policy', ['3 days of the proposal', '60 days of the medical examination', '1 year of the start date'],
 'The policy document is given within 30 days from issuance, meaning the application is approved and the full premium received.'),
('Under the LIA Code, the grace period to pay a premium after its due date is at least:',
 '30 days', ['7 days', '14 days', '90 days'],
 'The LIA Code gives at least 30 days after the due date, during which the policy continues to be valid.'),
('A premium remains unpaid after the grace period and the policy has enough cash value. Many insurers will then:',
 'Pay it by an automatic loan', ['Cancel all bonuses already added', 'Refund the cash value', 'Raise the sum assured'],
 'An automatic premium loan pays the overdue premium against the cash value, keeping the policy in force, with interest charged on the loan.'),
('A policy with an automatic premium loan ends when:',
 'The debt exceeds the cash value', ['The first loan is granted', 'Interest rates change', 'The grace period begins'],
 'The policy ceases once the total debt with interest is more than the cash value, and the insurer then has 14 days to tell the policyowner.'),
('A lapsed policy can usually be reinstated within about:',
 'Two years, on conditions', ['Two weeks, with no conditions', 'Ten years, at any time', 'Never once lapsed'],
 'Insurers usually allow reinstatement within a set period, often two years, if conditions such as evidence of health and payment of arrears are met.'),
('A policyholder converts a whole life policy to a paid-up policy. Afterwards, the cover:',
 'Continues for life, at a lower sum', ['Ends at once with a cash payout', 'Keeps the full sum for a short time', 'Rises by the cash value'],
 'Paid-up status stops premiums and keeps cover for the rest of the term, here whole of life, at a reduced sum assured. Keeping the full sum for a limited time is extended term insurance.'),
('Using the cash value to keep the full sum assured in force for a limited period is called:',
 'Extended term insurance', ['A reduced paid-up policy', 'A fund switch', 'An automatic premium loan'],
 'Extended term insurance keeps the original sum assured but only until a calculated end date, after which there is no cash value left.'),
('A policyowner assigns a life policy to a bank. For the assignment to count when a claim is paid, the owner must:',
 'Give the insurer written notice', ['Get approval from MAS first', 'Pay a new first premium', 'Make a trust nomination on it'],
 'An assignment must be notified to the insurer in writing; the insurer is not responsible for checking that the transfer is legally valid.'),
('Which of these policies cannot be assigned?',
 'A policy under a trust nomination', ['A cash-funded whole life policy', 'A policy with a revocable nominee', 'An endowment with a cash value'],
 'The LIA Code lists CPFIS, HPS, SRS, section 73 CLPA and trust nomination policies as ones the law does not allow to be transferred.'),
('Under the LIA Code, how much notice must an insurer give before it changes the interest rate on a policy loan?',
 'At least 30 days', ['At least 7 days', 'At least 90 days', 'No notice at all'],
 "The insurer states the rate when the loan is taken and gives at least 30 days' notice of any change, plus a statement of the amount owed once a year."),
('Under the LIA Code, written notice of a claim should be given:',
 'Within 30 days or as soon as possible', ['Within 7 days, or the claim becomes void', 'Within one year of the policy date', 'Only after a court order is made'],
 'The person claiming should notify the insurer in writing within 30 days of the event or as soon as possible.'),
('Once the insurer has full information for assessing a claim, the LIA Code gives it how long to accept or reject?',
 '21 days', ['7 days', '60 days', '6 months'],
 'Insurers commonly acknowledge a claim within 7 days, ask for more information within 14 days and decide within 21 days of having full information.'),
('An insurer pays a death claim more than two months after written notice of the death. Under the LIA Code it must:',
 'Add interest from the notice date', ['Waive any policy loans owed', 'Double the sum assured', 'Refund every premium paid as well'],
 'Interest runs from the notice date to payment, using the rate LIA publishes and updates every three months.'),
("The insured's age was understated at the start. On a claim, the benefit is generally:",
 'Recalculated for the true age', ['Paid in full as stated', 'Refused altogether', 'Increased by the difference'],
 'The insurer pays what the premiums paid would have bought at the true age. If the age was overstated, the excess premiums are refunded.'),
('The policy has been in force beyond its contestable period. The insurer may still void it if it proves:',
 'Fraud', ['A late premium', 'A change of address', 'A small typing error'],
 'After the set period, usually one or two years, the insurer cannot treat the policy as never issued, unless fraud is proven.'),
('Under the LIA Code, a claim cannot be rejected for non-disclosure unless the fact was asked, known to the proposer and:',
 'Material to the decision', ['Discovered after five years', 'Confirmed by MAS', 'Written by the agent'],
 'The fact must have been material, asked for in the application, known to the proposer and one the insurer would reasonably expect to be disclosed.'),
('A living benefit is paid on a policy bought with CPF savings. The law requires the money to go:',
 "Into the member's CPF account", ['To the member in cash', 'To the servicing agent', "Into the member's bank"],
 'For policies paid with CPF or SRS savings, any claim paid while the member is alive must be credited to the CPF or SRS account.'),
]
